WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.900
ok guys for today's problem we've got
this epoxide and we are going to react

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.360
with methoxide in methanol, we're going
to get this product so what we need to

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.250
do is show the mechanism. if this
confusing you will want to take a look at

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.250
my lecture on epoxides and then give
this a shot.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.220
ok guys so to draw this mechanism we
have to recall a couple things about

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.329
epoxides. number one: eooxides are very
susceptible to nucleophilic attack

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:48.700
because there's a lot of ring stream
from that small three-membered ring, that

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.130
means that either of these carbons could
potentially be attacked by a nucleophile

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.920
so we do have a nucleophile, we've got
methoxide, that's gonna be a good

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.870
nucleophile, so we want to ask ourselves:
which carbon is it gonna attack in the

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:04.289
epoxide? now sterics are going to
dictate that it is going to go over here

00:01:04.289 --> 00:01:09.630
it is the less sterically hindered side so
it is more available for nucleophilic

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:14.409
attack, so if it attacks right there, it's
gonna pop open the epoxide, we're

00:01:14.409 --> 00:01:19.229
gonna get this here, so this is the
methoxide that attacked, this used to be

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:23.360
the epoxide here but now that popped open
to leave the oxyanion

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.810
there and now the second step here is
kinda like the second step of Williamson

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.560
ether synthesis, except that it's
intramolecular because this oxyanion

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:38.320
can do an SN2 right here to kick off
this bromide and that'll lead us to the

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.610
product. and so what we can do just to
make sure we're able to draw that

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:48.620
properly, we can see that this is gonna
create a five-membered ring because this

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:53.030
oxyanion is going to attack this
carbon so that generates this

00:01:53.030 --> 00:02:00.640
five-membered ring, and then this group
over here will still be there so there

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:07.050
we go we have an opening of the epoxide
followed by intramolecular ring closure.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.880
thanks for watching, guys, subscribe to my channel for more tutorials and as always feel free to email me

